Brian Kernighan's algorithm:
```c
int countSetBits(int n) {
    int count = 0;
    while (n > 0) {
        n &= (n - 1); // Clears the LSB
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}
```
Each iteration of the loop clears the least significant set bit of `n`. The loop runs exactly `k` times, where `k` is the number of set bits in `n`.

**Efficiency:** This algorithm is more efficient than a simple right-shift loop (`n >>= 1; count += n & 1;`) which runs `log2(n)` times (for all bits). Kernighan's algorithm only iterates for each set bit, not for every bit position. For sparse numbers (few set bits), it's significantly faster.

The parity of the number of set bits (also known as the population count parity) can be found by repeatedly XORing halves of the number until only one bit remains. This works because XORing bits effectively sums them modulo 2.

For a 32-bit integer `x`:
```c
x ^= x >> 16;
x ^= x >> 8;
x ^= x >> 4;
x ^= x >> 2;
x ^= x >> 1;
return x & 1; // The LSB holds the parity
```
The final result `x & 1` will be 1 if the number of set bits was odd, and 0 if it was even.
